Id: logf011w
Severity: WARN
Category: logging
Check: check_logfiles
Fix: Install logrotate, name the files in /etc/logrotate.d, and enable logrotate.timer (or the cron.daily job).
References: logrotate(8); logrotate.conf(5); rsyslog.conf(5); syslog-ng.conf(5); CIS Distribution Independent Linux Benchmark 4.2.3 (Ensure logrotate is configured)
Controls: CIS 8.2, 8.3; NIST AU-9; ISO 8.15

A syslog daemon (rsyslog, syslog-ng or the classic syslogd) is
configured to write log files on this Linux host, and they are not
rotated: logrotate is not installed, or it has no /etc/logrotate.conf,
or no entry in it or in what it includes (/etc/logrotate.d) matches the
file, or nothing runs it (no logrotate.timer enabled, no cron.daily,
cron.d or crontab job). A log file that is never rotated grows until
the disk is full; a full /var stops the daemon, the services around it,
and the record of what happened. The detail says which of the four it
is and names the files.

Every distribution that ships rsyslog or syslog-ng ships a logrotate
entry for its files (/etc/logrotate.d/rsyslog, syslog-ng) and runs
logrotate from a timer or cron.daily, so a stock host says nothing; so
does a host that logs to the journal alone, which has no text logs to
rotate (journald bounds its own size with SystemMaxUse). Only files
that exist are judged: rsyslog creates a file at its first message,
and Debian's and Ubuntu's stock rules name one (/var/log/mail.err)
that their logrotate stanza leaves out, which on a host without mail
errors is never created; where it is, it is reported. A path with a
date or variable in it ($YEAR, %HOSTNAME%) makes a new file by itself
and is left out.

To fix it, install logrotate, put the files in a stanza under
/etc/logrotate.d (the syslog package's own is the model), and enable
the timer ('systemctl enable --now logrotate.timer') or restore
/etc/cron.daily/logrotate.
